  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 125 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThursday into Friday, a strong upper-level system will be positioned \nacross portions of the Midwest and southeastern United States. In \nthe wake of this system, northwesterly flow aloft will become \nestablished across the region as upper-level ridging builds into the \ncentral Plains. Anticyclonic vorticity advection and associated 500-\nmb height rises will favor increasing subsidence and a gradual \nwarming trend through the latter portion of the week. With 850-mb \ntemperatures returning to the 5 to 10\u00b0C range, highs should reach \nthe upper 40s Thanksgiving Day before warming into the lower to \nmiddle 50s Friday, which would be above normal for late November. \nIncreasing subsidence beneath the building ridge, along with a \nrelatively dry air mass in place, should support dry conditions for \nthe Thanksgiving holiday and Friday. A tightening pressure gradient \ncould support some gusty northwest winds, especially across portions \nof the Sandhills Friday afternoon. Overnight lows should generally \nremain in the upper teens to lower 20s both Thursday and Friday \nnights, with the dry air mass and relatively clear skies supporting \nefficient radiational cooling.\n\nThis weekend into early next week, a more active upper-level pattern \nlooks to develop as a northern-stream trough becomes established \nacross the north-central United States and Intermountain West. Model \nguidance suggests this trough may dig southward into portions of the \nwestern Rockies before ejecting eastward late Saturday into early \nSunday morning. However, differences remain between the GFS/GEFS and \nECMWF/EPS solutions regarding the strength and placement of the \nnorthern-stream trough, which will have implications on \nprecipitation chances across the forecast area. The GFS/GEFS \nmaintains the mid-level trough farther north and east, which would \nlimit moisture return into the region while keeping the stronger \nforcing for ascent displaced farther east. Conversely, the ECMWF/EPS \ndigs the trough farther west, favoring greater moisture return ahead \nof the system and increasing upper-level support for precipitation \ndevelopment across portions of the area Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. Given the continued disparity among model solutions, \nconfidence remains low regarding the coverage and timing of any \nprecipitation. Some light snow may be possible across western \nNebraska, though given the relatively marginal thermal profiles and \nlack of a significantly colder air mass, a rain/snow mix appears to \nbe the more likely scenario should precipitation develop. The \nevolution of this system will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles as confidence in the upper-level pattern \nincreases. Highs this weekend should generally remain in the middle \nto upper 40s, with overnight lows in the upper 20s to lower 30s.\n\nBeyond the weekend, a more active upper-level pattern looks to \npersist into early next week, potentially supporting additional \nprecipitation opportunities across the region. However, confidence \nremains low regarding the timing and evolution of individual \ndisturbances. Despite the potentially more active pattern, there \nremains little indication of a significant cold air intrusion \nthrough the end of the forecast period. Persistent troughing across \nthe Gulf of Alaska, along with the broader upper-level flow pattern, \nshould continue to favor the transport of relatively mild Pacific \nair into the CONUS. With little indication of high-latitude blocking \nor a pattern conducive to sustained southward transport of Arctic \nair into the central Plains, temperatures should generally remain \nnear to above normal through the end of the forecast period.",
